{"duration_api_ms":80734,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"2f796ddc-1aa8-4699-939d-0bfe0f8e9ffc","total_cost_usd":0.29550380000000004,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11033,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":10347,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6280},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11033,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10347,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11033,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11033},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":10347,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11033,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.29550380000000004,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":6280,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years. In fact, its encounter with Saturn was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible. The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2 began its long-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UTC Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition that began with Titania and Oberon, discovered in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at about 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Both documents carry the identical title; the base's is kept.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"a2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft name and number of prior encounters.\"},{\"segment\":\"a3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"In fact, its encounter with Saturn was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected the planet whose encounter enabled later flybys.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"reason\":\"Fixed typo; corrected next target, spacecraft and close-study duration.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2 began its long-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft, observation type and start date.\"},{\"segment\":\"a6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected the light-level factor.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UTC Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"reason\":\"Corrected time zone, year and swapped distance units.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition that began with Titania and Oberon, discovered in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at about 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon count, names, author, date, ring counts and field tilt.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected wind-speed units and ocean depth and description.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon names and that these are the larger moons.\"},{\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected mission duration to nearly a decade.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected Challenger crew count and launch date.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 3 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"No Voyager 3 flew; Voyager 2's primary mission was Jupiter and Saturn.\"},{\"statement\":\"In fact, its encounter with Saturn was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible.\",\"corrects\":\"its encounter with Jupiter was optimized\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"The Saturn flyby trajectory was chosen to permit the Uranus leg.\"},{\"statement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"corrects\":\"a future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days of close study\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Saturn was already visited; Neptune came next; Voyager 1 never reached Uranus.\"},{\"statement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2 began its long-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Observations must precede the Jan. 1986 flyby; Voyager 1 skipped Uranus.\"},{\"statement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"corrects\":\"five-hundred times less\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"At 19 AU sunlight is roughly 1/370 of Earth's, cited by NASA as 400.\"},{\"statement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UTC Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 50,640 miles (81,500 kilometers).\",\"corrects\":\"17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Flyby was 1986 at 17:59 UTC; 81,500 km equals 50,640 miles.\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition that began with Titania and Oberon, discovered in 1787), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at about 59 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1687), three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Standard Voyager 2 results; Uranus itself was unknown before 1781.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 450 miles per hour (724 kilometers per hour) and found evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 497 miles (800 kilometers) below the top cloud surface.\",\"corrects\":\"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers)\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Source unit pairs are internally inconsistent; 450 mph=724 km/h, 800 km=497 mi.\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\",\"corrects\":\"Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus’ smaller moons\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Titan orbits Saturn; these five are Uranus's major moons.\"},{\"statement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly decade-long travels.\",\"corrects\":\"nearly century-long travels\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Voyager 2 launched in 1977, under nine years before the 1986 flyby.\"},{\"statement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"corrects\":\"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Challenger broke up on Jan. 28, 1986, killing its crew of seven.\"}]}","ttft_ms":54047,"type":"result","duration_ms":80800,"uuid":"df13aa72-f344-46af-8b8f-717153b4c08e","ttft_stream_ms":1213,"time_to_request_ms":65,"first_content_frame_ms":1213,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
